Subject: [PATCH] fonts: supply captured generic identities through WebPreferences

Generic CSS families are selected from browser preference maps before
fontconfig sees the requested face. A fontconfig serif alias therefore cannot
replace Chrome's existing Times New Roman preference with the Mac's Times.

Read fonts.generic_family_map through the existing profile loader and set the
common-script maps where Chrome produces WebPreferences. Every renderer uses
the existing preference delivery. Named families, unknown generic settings and
unmeasured per-script maps retain their own behavior. No accessor is replaced.

Derivation requires a detected named control matching every captured metric
and the native preferred identity from Chromium's platform locale resources.
Widths alone do not identify a font. Standard/default and math remain absent
when unmeasured. This does not reconstruct a platform's character fallback.

Reference: resources/fingerprints/raw/m4-max-chrome-20260908T163229Z.json,
fonts.metrics and fonts.detected.

The Windows generic-family control this patch was written without now exists.
resources/fingerprints/raw/windows-chrome-20260910T140813Z.json measures all six
generic families at 40px next to 76 named families, which is exactly the pair of
inputs this patch says a derivation needs: each generic is identified by the
named family whose ten TextMetrics fields it matches, and the platform resource
says which family Chrome prefers there. The result is serif = Times New Roman,
sans-serif = Arial, monospace = Consolas, cursive = Comic Sans MS,
fantasy = Impact, system-ui = Segoe UI.

Five of the six agree with the platform resource. monospace does not, and the
disagreement is the resource's, not the capture's: IDS_FIXED_FONT_FAMILY in
locale_settings_win.grd is Courier New, but ShouldUseAlternateDefaultFixedFont
in prefs_tab_helper.cc substitutes IDS_FIXED_FONT_FAMILY_ALT_WIN, Consolas, for
any Courier default. The capture settles it: Courier New measures 160.02 wide
with fontBoundingBox 33/12, the monospace generic measures 149.96 with 37/10,
and Consolas measures 149.96 with 37/10. Reading the .grd alone would have put
the wrong family in a Windows profile, which is why the measurement was worth
waiting for rather than guessing.

Two generics stay out of the map by decision, not for want of evidence. `math`
is not measured by the capture, so only one of the two required inputs exists;
Cambria Math has its own row at 153.73 with 31/9 but nothing attributes it to
the generic. `system-ui` does not travel through WebPreferences at all -- Blink
takes it from FontCache::SystemFontFamily -- so this emitter is the wrong place
for it and setting a serif/sans map entry would not move it.

The default (standard) family needs no change, and the capture proves it rather
than assuming it. On Windows an absent family falls through to the standard
font, and every family the reference host lacks -- Menlo, Zapfino, PingFang SC,
Optima, Papyrus and the rest -- measures identically to the serif generic, so
the standard font there is Times New Roman. IDS_STANDARD_FONT_FAMILY on Linux is
also Times New Roman, so the two already agree and there is nothing to select.

Those six identities belong in the profile catalogue behind its existing
requires gate, not in a code default. A Windows profile that names Consolas on
a host without Consolas is worse than one that names nothing: the fixed
generic then falls through to the standard font and renders every <pre> and
every monospace run in Times New Roman, which is both a layout break and a
louder tell than the host's own monospace face. Presence has to gate the claim,
and resources/profiles/dispersion/font_packs.json already gates packs that way.

Primary source at Chromium 79460ebecaa5625e57a5fb679a735659e73dc687:
- chrome/app/resources/locale_settings_{mac,win,linux}.grd: the preferred
  identities.
- chrome/browser/ui/prefs/prefs_tab_helper.cc:
  ShouldUseAlternateDefaultFixedFont.
- chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc:
  ChromeContentBrowserClient::OverrideWebPreferences.

Ledger: fonts.generic-families. V1 and browser controls remain required.

